3. Necessity of FV‑Greedy Selection

We establish that FV‑maximization is not a heuristic but a necessary property of any optimal policy.

Lemma 1 (Existence and uniqueness of V^*).
Define the operator T: C_b(\mathcal{S}) \to C_b(\mathcal{S}) by

(T V)(s) = \max\Bigl\{ U(s),\; \max_{o\in\mathcal{O}_0} \bigl( \gamma^{\tau(s,o)} \mathbb{E}[V(s') \mid s,o] - c(s,o) \bigr) \Bigr\}.

Since \tau(s,o) \ge 1, we have \|T V_1 - T V_2\|_\infty \le \gamma \|V_1 - V_2\|_\infty. By the Banach fixed‑point theorem (see e.g., Puterman, 1994), T possesses a unique fixed point V^* \in C_b(\mathcal{S}). This V^* is precisely the optimal state‑value function.

Theorem 1 (Necessity of FV‑greedy meta‑policy).
Any policy \pi attaining V^\pi(s) = V^*(s) for all s must, at every state visited with positive probability, select an action (stop or an internal operation) that is FV‑greedy, i.e.,

\pi(s) \in \arg\max\bigl\{0,\; \max_{o} \mathrm{FV}(o; s) \bigr\}.

Proof.
Sufficiency: If a policy is FV‑greedy everywhere, then its value function satisfies the same fixed‑point equation (1) that defines V^*; by uniqueness it must equal V^*.

Necessity: Suppose there exists a state s_0 with positive occupancy under \pi where \pi selects an operation o such that \mathrm{FV}(o; s_0) < \max\{0, \max_{o'} \mathrm{FV}(o'; s_0)\}. Then a one‑step deviation to the FV‑greedy choice at s_0 would strictly improve the value at s_0 (by construction of V^*), contradicting the optimality of \pi. ∎

Thus every bounded‑optimal agent must internally evaluate operations by their Future Value.

