4. Composition and the Regret Decomposition

Sequential composition. Given a finite sequence \mathbf{o} = (o_1, \dots, o_k) of operations, the composed operation \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o}) is defined by the concatenated transition kernel and accumulated cost and duration:

T_{\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o})}(s_k \mid s_0) = \int \cdots \int \prod_{i=1}^k T_{o_i}(s_i \mid s_{i-1})\, ds_1 \cdots ds_{k-1},

\tau(s_0, \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o})) = \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}\bigl[ \tau(S_{i-1}, o_i) \mid s_0 \bigr], \qquad
c(s_0, \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o})) = \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}\bigl[ c(S_{i-1}, o_i) \mid s_0 \bigr],

where S_0 = s_0 and S_i \sim T_{o_i}(\cdot \mid S_{i-1}). The composed operation belongs to the set of all operations \mathcal{O} if the latter is closed under such concatenations (e.g., all finite sequences of primitives).

The value of a composed operation is not the sum of the individual FVs; instead, it admits a robust performance‑difference decomposition.

Theorem 2 (Regret decomposition).
Let \mathbf{o} = (o_1, \dots, o_k) and define the discounted cumulative time D_{i-1} = \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \tau(S_{j-1}, o_j) (with D_0 = 0). For each step, define the instantaneous regret of choosing o_i at S_{i-1} relative to the optimal continuation:

\mathrm{Regret}_i(s) = V^*(s) - Q(s, o_i) \ge 0.

Then the Future Value of the composed operation satisfies

\mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o}); s) = \bigl[V^*(s) - U(s)\bigr] \;-\; \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}\!\Bigl[ \gamma^{D_{i-1}} \,\mathrm{Regret}_i(S_{i-1}) \;\Big|\; s \Bigr].
\tag{3}

Proof.
Telescope the identity \gamma^{D_k} V^*(S_k) - V^*(s) = \sum_{i=1}^k \bigl( \gamma^{D_i} V^*(S_i) - \gamma^{D_{i-1}} V^*(S_{i-1}) \bigr). By the definition of Q,

\gamma^{D_i} V^*(S_i) - \gamma^{D_{i-1}} V^*(S_{i-1}) = \gamma^{D_{i-1}} \bigl( \gamma^{\tau(S_{i-1},o_i)} V^*(S_i) - V^*(S_{i-1}) \bigr).

Taking conditional expectations and using Q(S_{i-1}, o_i) = \gamma^{\tau(S_{i-1},o_i)} \mathbb{E}[V^*(S_i) \mid S_{i-1}, o_i] - c(S_{i-1}, o_i), we obtain

\mathbb{E}\bigl[ \gamma^{D_i} V^*(S_i) - \gamma^{D_{i-1}} V^*(S_{i-1}) \mid S_{i-1} \bigr] = \gamma^{D_{i-1}} \bigl( Q(S_{i-1}, o_i) + c(S_{i-1}, o_i) - V^*(S_{i-1}) \bigr).

Since Q(s, o_i) - V^*(s) = -\mathrm{Regret}_i(s), summing over i and incorporating the cost terms yields

\mathbb{E}[\gamma^{D_k} V^*(S_k) \mid s] - V^*(s) = -\sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}\bigl[ \gamma^{D_{i-1}} (\mathrm{Regret}_i(S_{i-1}) + c(S_{i-1}, o_i)) \mid s \bigr].

The total cost of the composite operation is \sum_i \mathbb{E}[ \gamma^{D_{i-1}} c(S_{i-1}, o_i) \mid s] (costs are not discounted again because they are already linear). Substituting into \mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o});s) = \mathbb{E}[\gamma^{\tau_{\text{tot}}} V^*(S_k) \mid s] - \text{total cost} - U(s) and identifying \gamma^{\tau_{\text{tot}}} = \gamma^{D_k} gives (3). ∎

Corollary 2a (Optimal sequences are trivial).
If each o_i is FV‑optimal in the state it is applied (i.e., \mathrm{Regret}_i = 0), then \mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o}); s) = V^*(s) - U(s). Hence, when every component is optimal, the composed operation confers no extra benefit over stopping and acting optimally after the first step.

Corollary 2b (Error propagation).
If for all i we have \mathrm{Regret}_i \le \varepsilon almost surely, then

\mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o}); s) \ge V^*(s) - U(s) - \varepsilon \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}[\gamma^{D_{i-1}} \mid s].

This bound is directly useful for the meta‑cognitive algorithm: it limits how estimation errors in individual FV estimates degrade the realised FV of a composite.

