5. Hierarchical Emergence of Irreducible Operations

Although composed operations do not enjoy additive FV, they can generate genuinely novel FV profiles—equivalence classes not realizable by any shallower composition. We formalise this as a generic property.

Definition (Operation equivalence).
Two operations o, o' are equivalent, written o \sim o', if \mathrm{FV}(o; s) = \mathrm{FV}(o'; s) for all s \in \mathcal{S}. Let [o] denote the equivalence class and \Gamma(\mathcal{O}) the set of equivalence classes of operations in \mathcal{O}.

Composition hierarchy.
\mathcal{O}_0 is the finite set of primitive operations. For k \ge 0,

\mathcal{O}_{k+1} = \mathcal{O}_k \;\cup\; \bigl\{ \mathcal{C}(o_1, \dots, o_m) : o_i \in \mathcal{O}_k,\; 1 \le m \le K \bigr\},

where K \ge 2 is a fixed maximum arity (typically K=2).

Axiom (Non‑Degeneracy).
For each depth k, there exists a set of n^k probe states \{p_1, \dots, p_{n^k}\} such that the Hankel matrix

H_k[w, j] = \mathrm{FV}(\text{word } w; \; p_j), \qquad w \in \mathcal{O}_0^k,

has full row rank n^k, where each word w = (o_{i_1}, \dots, o_{i_k}) denotes the composed operation formed by that sequence.

In other words, the FV functions of distinct length‑k sequences are linearly independent when evaluated on a suitable finite set of states. This condition is generic: if the transition kernels and the external MDP are parametrised by a finite‑dimensional real‑analytic family, the determinant of any square submatrix of H_k is a real‑analytic function of the parameters. By the identity theorem, such a determinant is either identically zero or non‑zero on a set of full measure. Hence, full row rank holds for almost all environments; degeneracy is a knife‑edge case.

Theorem 3 (Unbounded emergence).
Under the Non‑Degeneracy Axiom, for every k \ge 1,

|\Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k)| \ge n^k,

and consequently \Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k) \setminus \Gamma(\mathcal{O}_{k-1}) \neq \emptyset. The number of distinct equivalence classes grows without bound as k \to \infty.

Proof.
The \mathcal{O}_k hierarchy contains all length‑k sequences of primitives (by pairwise composition). By the axiom, the n^k FV functions corresponding to these sequences are linearly independent, hence distinct. Therefore |\Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k)| \ge n^k. Since |\mathcal{O}_{k-1}| \le \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} n^i = (n^k - 1)/(n-1) < n^k for n \ge 2, the pigeonhole principle guarantees new classes at depth k. ∎

This theorem establishes that an FV‑maximising agent endowed with even a small set of primitives will inevitably generate an unbounded repertoire of genuinely novel cognitive operations, provided the environment is generic. No external designer needs to specify these compositions; they emerge automatically from the pressure to maximise FV.

