6. Meta‑Cognitive Algorithm and Sample Complexity

Algorithm. The agent maintains a library \mathbb{O} of known operations (initially \mathcal{O}_0). At each decision point with internal state s:

1. Evaluation: For each o \in \mathbb{O}, estimate \widehat{\mathrm{FV}}(o; s) using N Monte‑Carlo rollouts of the internal world model (or real experience).
2. Selection: If \max_{o\in\mathbb{O}} \widehat{\mathrm{FV}}(o; s) > 0, execute o^* = \arg\max and continue; else stop and act externally.
3. Composition proposal: With small probability \varepsilon, sample a few operations o_1, \dots, o_m \in \mathbb{O}, create o_{\text{new}} = \mathcal{C}(o_1, \dots, o_m), and insert into \mathbb{O}.
4. Pruning: Periodically remove operations whose upper confidence bound on average FV is non‑positive.

Sample complexity. Assume \mathcal{S} is finite with M = |\mathcal{S}| and FV values bounded in [-B,B]. Evaluating a depth‑k composed operation requires simulating up to k internal steps.

Theorem 4 (Sample complexity).
For any \varepsilon, \delta > 0, with probability at least 1-\delta the above algorithm learns an \varepsilon-optimal meta‑policy over all operations in \mathcal{O}_K (depth \le K) using

O\!\left( \frac{B^2 n^K K M}{\varepsilon^2} \log\frac{n^K M}{\delta} \right)

samples from the generative model of internal transitions.

Proof sketch. There are at most n^K candidate operations. For each (s, o) pair, Hoeffding’s inequality shows that O(B^2/\varepsilon^2 \log(1/\delta')) rollouts suffice to estimate \mathrm{FV}(o; s) within \varepsilon/2. Union‑bounding over all pairs gives the result. The bound uses the generative model; actual runtime is multiplied by the average sequence length K. Corollary 2b ensures that even if individual FV estimates contain error, the realised composite FV degrades gracefully. ∎

For continuous state spaces with metric d and Lipschitz constants L for V^*, U, c, the same bound holds with M replaced by the covering number \mathcal{N}(\varepsilon/(2L)) (Kolmogorov–Tikhomirov). The additive discretisation error is absorbed into the \varepsilon tolerance.

