7. Unification of Perception, Reasoning, and Memory

All previously separate theories are exact instances of FV.

Perception (decision‑centric rate‑distortion).
A perception operation o_{\text{perc}} acquires an observation y with encoding cost \lambda I(X;Y \mid s). Its duration is the acquisition time. The FV is

\mathrm{FV}(o_{\text{perc}}; s) = \gamma^\tau \mathbb{E}_y[V^*(s \oplus y)] - U(s) - \lambda I(X;Y|s),

exactly the decision‑centric rate‑distortion objective.

Reasoning (Future Reasoning Value).
A reasoning step o_{\text{reason}} refines the internal state via a chain‑of‑thought token. Its cost is the FLOP count, duration the number of reasoning steps. The FV reduces to the Future Reasoning Value, and optimal stopping occurs when \max_o \mathrm{FRV} \le 0.

Memory (Future Memory Value).
A storage operation o_{\text{mem}} writes a tuple m to an external memory with cost c_{\text{store}} and duration. The FV becomes the Future Memory Value:

\mathrm{FMV} = \gamma^\tau \mathbb{E}[V^*(s \cup \{m\})] - U(s) - c_{\text{store}}.

Cross‑modal sequences. Because all operations are evaluated in the common FV currency, sequences such as perceive‑then‑reason are simply composed operations whose value is given by the regret decomposition (3). The framework does not promise automatic super‑additivity; rather, it allows the agent to discover beneficial sequences when the environment makes them worthwhile. Under the non‑degeneracy condition, many such sequences yield genuinely new cognitive behaviors.

