A. Duration‑Aware Model and Existence of V^*

The original model treated internal operations as instantaneous, which left the contraction property of the Bellman operator unjustified. We introduce operation durations to restore a rigorous fixed‑point argument.

Definition A.1 (Operation duration).
Every operation o is equipped with a stopping time \tau(s,o) : \mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{O} \to \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 1} such that \tau(s,o) \ge 1 almost surely and \mathbb{E}[\tau(s,o)] < \infty. For a composite operation \mathcal{C}(o_1,\dots,o_k) with state trajectory S_0, S_1, \dots, S_k,

\tau(s_0, \mathcal{C}(o_1,\dots,o_k)) = \sum_{i=1}^k \tau(S_{i-1}, o_i).

The duration represents the number of discrete world‑clock ticks consumed by the operation. The environment discount factor \gamma \in [0,1) applies to each tick.

Definition A.2 (Semi‑Markov Bellman operator).
Define the operator T on the space of bounded measurable functions V : \mathcal{S} \to \mathbb{R} by

(TV)(s) = \max\Bigl\{
U(s),\; \max_{o \in \mathcal{O}_0}
\bigl( \mathbb{E}[ \gamma^{\tau(s,o)} V(s') \mid s, o ] - c(s,o) \bigr)
\Bigr\},
\tag{A.1}

where U(s) = \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \mathbb{E}[R^e(x,a) \mid s] and c(s,o) \ge 0 is the computational cost of o.

Lemma A.1 (Contraction property).
If \tau(s,o) \ge 1 a.s. for all s,o, then T is a \gamma-contraction on (C_b(\mathcal{S}), \|\cdot\|_\infty).

Proof.
For any V_1, V_2 \in C_b(\mathcal{S}) and any o \in \mathcal{O}_0,

\begin{aligned}
\bigl| \mathbb{E}[ \gamma^{\tau(s,o)} V_1(s') \mid s, o ] - \mathbb{E}[ \gamma^{\tau(s,o)} V_2(s') \mid s, o ] \bigr|
&\le \mathbb{E}[ \gamma^{\tau(s,o)} \mid s, o ] \cdot \|V_1 - V_2\|_\infty \\
&\le \gamma \cdot \|V_1 - V_2\|_\infty,
\end{aligned}

because \tau \ge 1 implies \gamma^\tau \le \gamma pointwise. The inequality |\max_i a_i - \max_i b_i| \le \max_i |a_i - b_i| extends this to the maximum over the finite set of options (including the V-independent term U(s)). Hence \|TV_1 - TV_2\|_\infty \le \gamma \|V_1 - V_2\|_\infty. ∎

Theorem A.2 (Existence and uniqueness of V^*).
Under the assumptions of Lemma A.1, the operator T possesses a unique fixed point V^* \in C_b(\mathcal{S}), which is the optimal state‑value function. Moreover, V^* can be obtained as the uniform limit of value iteration starting from any bounded initial function.

Proof.
This is a direct application of the Banach fixed‑point theorem (Puterman, 1994, Thm. 6.2.3) to the contraction T. ∎

