C. Regret Decomposition for Composed Operations

The claim that the FV of a sequence equals the sum of the component FVs is false in general. We replace it with a correct regret decomposition that is central to understanding composite values.

Definition C.1 (Regret).
For any operation o and state s, the instantaneous regret of choosing o at s is

\mathrm{Regret}(o; s) := V^*(s) - Q(s,o) \ge 0.

Equivalently, \mathrm{FV}(o; s) = [V^*(s) - U(s)] - \mathrm{Regret}(o; s).

Theorem C.1 (Regret decomposition for sequences).
Let \mathbf{o} = (o_1, \dots, o_k) be a sequence of operations. Denote S_0 = s, S_i \sim T_{o_i}(\cdot \mid S_{i-1}), and cumulative discounted times D_{i-1} = \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \tau(S_{j-1}, o_j) with D_0 = 0. Then

\mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o}); s) = \bigl[V^*(s) - U(s)\bigr]
\;-\; \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}\Bigl[ \gamma^{D_{i-1}} \, \mathrm{Regret}(o_i; S_{i-1}) \Bigm| s \Bigr].
\tag{C.1}

Proof.
We proceed by induction on k.

Base case k = 1: Q(s, o_1) = V^*(s) - \mathrm{Regret}(o_1; s) by definition, so \mathrm{FV}(o_1; s) = [V^*(s) - U(s)] - \mathrm{Regret}(o_1; s), matching (C.1).

Inductive step: Assume the formula holds for all sequences of length k-1. Separate the first operation:

Q\bigl(s, \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o})\bigr) = \mathbb{E}\Bigl[ \gamma^{\tau_1} Q\bigl(S_1, \mathcal{C}(o_2,\dots,o_k)\bigr) \Bigm| s, o_1 \Bigr] - c(s, o_1),
\tag{C.2}

where \tau_1 = \tau(s, o_1). This identity follows by expanding the nested expectations and using the definition of Q for the tail sequence, exactly as shown in the main text’s Appendix C. Applying the induction hypothesis to the tail,

Q(S_1, \mathcal{C}(o_2,\dots,o_k)) = V^*(S_1) - \sum_{i=2}^k \mathbb{E}\Bigl[ \gamma^{D'_{i-1}} \mathrm{Regret}(o_i; S_{i-1}) \Bigm| S_1 \Bigr],

where D'_{i-1} = \sum_{j=2}^{i} \tau(S_{j-1}, o_j) is the elapsed time of the tail. Substitute into (C.2):

\begin{aligned}
Q(s, \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o})) &= \mathbb{E}\Bigl[ \gamma^{\tau_1} V^*(S_1) \Bigm| s, o_1 \Bigr]
- \sum_{i=2}^k \mathbb{E}\Bigl[ \gamma^{\tau_1} \gamma^{D'_{i-1}} \mathrm{Regret}(o_i; S_{i-1}) \Bigm| s \Bigr] - c(s, o_1) \\
&= Q(s, o_1) - \sum_{i=2}^k \mathbb{E}\Bigl[ \gamma^{D_{i-1}} \mathrm{Regret}(o_i; S_{i-1}) \Bigm| s \Bigr],
\end{aligned}

because \gamma^{\tau_1} \gamma^{D'_{i-1}} = \gamma^{D_{i-1}}. Finally, using Q(s, o_1) = V^*(s) - \mathrm{Regret}(o_1; s) and subtracting U(s) yields (C.1). ∎

Corollary C.2 (Optimal sequences).
If each operation o_i is FV‑optimal in its respective state (i.e., \mathrm{Regret}(o_i; S_{i-1}) = 0 a.s.), then \mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o}); s) = V^*(s) - U(s). Hence a sequence of individually optimal steps offers no advantage over stopping after the first optimal operation.

Corollary C.3 (Error propagation under suboptimal choices).
If \mathrm{Regret}(o_i; S_{i-1}) \le \varepsilon almost surely for all i, then

\mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o}); s) \ge [V^*(s) - U(s)] - \varepsilon \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}[ \gamma^{D_{i-1}} \mid s] \ge [V^*(s) - U(s)] - k\varepsilon.

This bound is used in the analysis of the meta‑cognitive algorithm (§G) to translate per‑step estimation errors into guarantees on the realized composite FV.

