D. Hierarchical Emergence of Irreducible Operations

The original emergence proof relied on an invalid strict‑improvement argument. We reconstruct it on a rigorous linear‑algebraic foundation, plus a weak alternative.

Definition D.1 (Operation equivalence).
Two operations o, o' are equivalent, o \sim o', if \mathrm{FV}(o; s) = \mathrm{FV}(o'; s) for all s \in \mathcal{S}. Let \Gamma(\mathcal{O}) be the set of equivalence classes.

Composition hierarchy.
\mathcal{O}_0 = \{a_1,\dots,a_n\} is the set of primitives. For k \ge 0,

\mathcal{O}_{k+1} = \mathcal{O}_k \cup \bigl\{ \mathcal{C}(o_1,\dots,o_m) : o_i \in \mathcal{O}_k,\; 1 \le m \le K \bigr\},

with maximum arity K \ge 2 (typically K=2).

D.1. Non‑Degeneracy and the Hankel Matrix

Fix a set of probe states P_k \subset \mathcal{S} with |P_k| = n^k. The Hankel matrix of depth k is

H_k \in \mathbb{R}^{P_k \times \mathcal{O}_0^k}, \qquad
(H_k)_{p,w} = \mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(w); p),

where each word w = o_{i_1} \dots o_{i_k} \in \mathcal{O}_0^k denotes the composed operation.

Axiom D.1 (Non‑Degeneracy).
For every k \ge 1, the matrix H_k has full column rank n^k.

Full column rank means that the n^k FV functions corresponding to the length‑k sequences, when restricted to the n^k probe states, are linearly independent. This is the natural requirement that distinct sequences generically produce distinct valuation profiles.

Theorem D.1 (Emergence of new classes).
Under Axiom D.1, for every k \ge 1,

|\Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k)| \ge n^k,

and consequently \Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k) \setminus \Gamma(\mathcal{O}_{k-1}) \neq \emptyset. The number of distinct equivalence classes grows without bound as k \to \infty.

Proof.
Since H_k has full column rank, its n^k columns are distinct as functions on P_k. Hence the corresponding operations \mathcal{C}(w) belong to n^k distinct equivalence classes, yielding |\Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k)| \ge n^k.

Now \mathcal{O}_{k-1} contains at most \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} n^i = (n^k-1)/(n-1) operations, which is strictly less than n^k for n \ge 2. By the pigeonhole principle, \mathcal{O}_k must contain equivalence classes not present in \mathcal{O}_{k-1}. ∎

D.2. Genericity of Non‑Degeneracy

We justify Axiom D.1 by showing it is the typical case in any smoothly parametrised family of environments.

Proposition D.2 (Genericity).
Assume the environment’s transition kernel P^e, reward R^e, and all primitive operation kernels T_{a_i} and costs c(\cdot, a_i) depend real‑analytically on a parameter \theta from a connected open set \Theta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d. Then the set of parameters for which Axiom D.1 fails (i.e., some H_k is rank‑deficient) is a closed subset of \Theta of Lebesgue measure zero.

Proof sketch.
Since the Bellman operator T_\theta in (A.1) is a uniformly \gamma-contraction whose components depend analytically on \theta, the unique fixed point V^*_\theta is a real‑analytic function of \theta (for finite state spaces this is standard; for continuous spaces the same holds under mild regularity via the analytic implicit function theorem in Banach spaces). Consequently, every entry of H_k(\theta)—which is a composition of V^*_\theta, expectations, and costs—is real‑analytic in \theta. The determinant of any n^k \times n^k square submatrix of H_k is therefore a real‑analytic function. By the identity theorem for real‑analytic functions, this determinant is either identically zero on \Theta or vanishes only on a closed, measure‑zero subset.

It remains to exhibit a single parameter \theta_0 where the determinant is non‑zero. Such a witness is easily constructed: choose an environment in which the internal state contains a perfect memory register that records the sequence of the last k operations as a distinct symbol, and let the terminal utility U(s) be an injective function of this memory. Then each length‑k sequence produces a different final state and hence a different FV on a suitable probe set, ensuring full rank. Because the family is assumed rich enough to include this case, the determinant cannot be identically zero. Thus, full rank holds for almost all \theta. ∎

This result upgrades the earlier informal “genericity” remark into a precise statement: degeneracy is a knife‑edge phenomenon.

D.3. Weak Emergence without Strong Genericity

If one objects to the full Non‑Degeneracy Axiom, a much weaker condition—the original “contextual dependence” (Condition 1 of the main text)—still implies unbounded growth when coupled with a mild recurrence hypothesis, though the growth rate is no longer \Omega(n^k).

Theorem D.3 (Weak emergence under \varphi-irreducibility).
Assume the existence of primitives a,b and a state s_0 such that

\Delta := \mathbb{E}[\mathrm{FV}(b; S) \mid s_0, a] - \mathrm{FV}(b; s_0) > 0,

and suppose the state space is \varphi-irreducible under some policy: there exist m_0 \in \mathbb{N}, a sequence r \in \mathcal{O}_0^{m_0}, and \delta > 0 such that for every reachable state s,

\mathbb{P}(S_{m_0} = s_0 \mid S_0 = s, \text{sequence } r) \ge \delta.

Then for every k \ge 1 there exists an operation of depth at most (m_0+2)k whose FV equivalence class is not representable by any shallower operation. Hence \lim_{k\to\infty} |\Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k)| = \infty.

Proof sketch.
Consider the composite operation w_k = (r, a, b)^k (i.e., repeat the block r,a,b k times). Because the regeneration block r reaches s_0 with probability at least \delta and the pair a,b then provides a synergy \Delta, a calculation using the regret decomposition (C.1) shows that \mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(w_k); s) differs from the best value achievable by any composition of depth strictly less than k by at least c \cdot (\delta \gamma^{(m_0+2)})^k \Delta > 0 for all k, guaranteeing a new equivalence class at each multiple of (m_0+2). This yields unbounded emergence, albeit with a weaker density than the exponential lower bound of Theorem D.1. ∎

