E. Sample Complexity Bounds

The meta‑cognitive algorithm of the main text needs a guarantee on the number of samples required to evaluate operations accurately. We provide bounds for both finite and continuous state spaces.

Assumption E.1. FV values are bounded: |\mathrm{FV}(o; s)| \le B for all s, o. The world model is a generative oracle that, given (s,o), returns a sample of s' \sim T_o(\cdot \mid s) and the cost c(s,o) and duration \tau(s,o).

E.1. Finite State Space

Theorem E.1 (Finite state space).
Let |\mathcal{S}| = M. For any \varepsilon, \delta > 0, an \varepsilon-optimal meta‑policy over all operations in \mathcal{O}_K can be learned with probability at least 1-\delta using

O\!\left( \frac{B^2 \, n^K K M}{\varepsilon^2} \log \frac{n^K M}{\delta} \right)

calls to the generative model.

Proof.
The number of candidate operations up to depth K is at most N_{\text{ops}} \le n^K. For each operation o and state s, we estimate \mathrm{FV}(o; s) by averaging m independent rollouts: simulate the sequence (costing up to K steps each), compute the terminal value via the world model, subtract cost and U(s). Each rollout yields an unbiased estimate bounded in [-B,B]. By Hoeffding’s inequality, m = O\!\bigl( \frac{B^2}{\varepsilon^2} \log\frac{1}{\delta'} \bigr) ensures | \widehat{\mathrm{FV}} - \mathrm{FV} | \le \varepsilon with probability at least 1-\delta'. Union‑bounding over all N_{\text{ops}} M pairs, set \delta' = \delta/(N_{\text{ops}} M). The total number of simulated internal steps is at most N_{\text{ops}} M \cdot m \cdot K, giving the claimed bound. ∎

E.2. Continuous State Space with Lipschitz Continuity

Assumption E.2. The functions V^*, U, c(\cdot, o) are L-Lipschitz with respect to a metric d on \mathcal{S}.

Theorem E.2 (Continuous state space).
Let \mathcal{N}(\eta) be the \eta-covering number of (\mathcal{S}, d). Under Assumption E.2, the sample complexity to achieve \varepsilon-accuracy uniformly over \mathcal{S} and \mathcal{O}_K is

O\!\left( \frac{B^2 \, n^K K \, \mathcal{N}(\varepsilon/(4L))}{\varepsilon^2}
\log\frac{n^K \mathcal{N}(\varepsilon/(4L))}{\delta} \right).

Proof.
Fix \eta = \varepsilon/(4L). Let \tilde{\mathcal{S}} be an \eta-net of \mathcal{S}. For any s \in \mathcal{S}, let \hat{s} \in \tilde{\mathcal{S}} be its nearest net point. For any operation o,

\begin{aligned}
|\mathrm{FV}(o; s) - \mathrm{FV}(o; \hat{s})|
&\le |\mathbb{E}[\gamma^\tau V^*(s') \mid s,o] - \mathbb{E}[\gamma^\tau V^*(\hat{s}') \mid \hat{s},o]|
+ |c(s,o)-c(\hat{s},o)| + |U(s)-U(\hat{s})| \\
&\le L\, d(s,\hat{s}) + L\, \mathbb{E}[d(s',\hat{s}')] + L\, d(s,\hat{s}) \le 4L\eta = \varepsilon,
\end{aligned}

assuming the internal transitions are 1‑Lipschitz in state (a mild regularity condition; otherwise an extra Lipschitz constant appears but the proof extends). Now apply Theorem E.1 with the finite state set \tilde{\mathcal{S}} of size |\tilde{\mathcal{S}}| \le \mathcal{N}(\eta) to obtain estimates accurate to \varepsilon/2 on \tilde{\mathcal{S}}. By the triangle inequality, this yields \varepsilon-accuracy everywhere. Substituting \eta = \varepsilon/(4L) yields the bound. ∎

