F. Unification: Perception, Reasoning, and Memory as Special Cases

We verify that the three previously separate theories are exact instantiations of the FV framework.

Perception (decision‑centric rate‑distortion).
A perception operation o_{\text{perc}} acquires a sensor observation y and updates the internal state to s' = s \oplus y. The encoding incurs an information‑theoretic cost c = \lambda I(X; Y \mid s) and duration \tau = 1. Then

\mathrm{FV}(o_{\text{perc}}; s) = \gamma \, \mathbb{E}_y[V^*(s \oplus y)] - U(s) - \lambda I(X;Y \mid s),

which is exactly the objective of decision‑centric rate‑distortion theory.

Reasoning (Future Reasoning Value).
A reasoning step o_{\text{reason}} applies a transformer layer, producing a new state s' with cost c (FLOPs) and duration \tau = 1. Its FV is

\mathrm{FRV}(o_{\text{reason}}; s) = \gamma \, \mathbb{E}[V^*(s') \mid s] - U(s) - c.

The optimal stopping rule is to cease reasoning when \max_o \mathrm{FRV} \le 0.

Memory (Future Memory Value).
Storing an item m costs c_{\text{store}} and takes one tick. The new state is s \cup \{m\}. Thus,

\mathrm{FMV}(o_{\text{mem}}; s) = \gamma \, \mathbb{E}[V^*(s \cup \{m\})] - U(s) - c_{\text{store}}.

Only memories with positive FMV are admitted.

All three follow directly from Definition B.1 with the appropriate operational semantics.

