G. Meta‑Cognitive Algorithm: Details and Rationale

We provide the full algorithm and discuss why composition proposal is beneficial despite the regret decomposition showing that a sequence of optimal steps yields no extra value.

G.1. Algorithm Pseudocode

State: library \mathbb{O} (initially \mathcal{O}_0), current internal state s.

Loop:

1. Estimate FV: For each o \in \mathbb{O}, obtain \widehat{\mathrm{FV}}(o; s) by N Monte‑Carlo rollouts (using the world model) or from a cached value‑function approximator.
2. Selection: Let o^* = \arg\max_{o \in \mathbb{O}} \widehat{\mathrm{FV}}(o; s). If \widehat{\mathrm{FV}}(o^*; s) > 0, execute o^*, update s \leftarrow new state, and continue. Else, stop and take the external action \arg\max_a \mathbb{E}[R^e \mid s].
3. Composition proposal: With small probability \varepsilon_{\text{prop}}, randomly select a few operations from \mathbb{O} (biased toward those with recent high FV), form their sequential composition o_{\text{new}} = \mathcal{C}(o_{i_1}, \dots, o_{i_m}), and add it to \mathbb{O}.
4. Pruning: Periodically, for each o \in \mathbb{O}, maintain a running average of \widehat{\mathrm{FV}}. If the upper confidence bound (e.g., mean + 2 standard errors) is \le 0, remove o.

G.2. Why Compose? The Role of Bounded Meta‑Reasoning

Theorem C.1 shows that if each step is chosen optimally, a pre‑compiled sequence has FV equal to that of a single optimal operation. In an agent with unlimited, instantaneous meta‑reasoning, there is no incentive to compose. In practice, however, online meta‑reasoning is costly: the agent must estimate FV for every candidate operation at each decision point, consuming samples and wall‑clock time.

Let \kappa > 0 denote the per‑step overhead of running the meta‑controller (evaluating \widehat{\mathrm{FV}} for the full library). Executing a pre‑compiled macro o = \mathcal{C}(o_1,\dots,o_k) as a single chunk incurs the meta‑cost only once, whereas executing the sequence step‑by‑step with re‑optimisation incurs the meta‑cost k times. The effective FV of a macro, accounting for the saved meta‑cost, becomes

\mathrm{FV}_{\kappa}(o; s) = \mathrm{FV}(o; s) + (k-1)\kappa.

Even if the per‑step regret is positive, a macro may have a higher effective FV than the step‑by‑step policy if the saved overhead outweighs the suboptimality.

The composition proposal mechanism therefore serves to compile frequently executed sequences into chunks that reduce online planning burden. Over time, the agent builds a library of skills whose value reflects both task performance and computational efficiency. The regret decomposition (Corollary C.3) guarantees that the error introduced by using a macro (due to imperfect step choices) is bounded and gracefully degrades.

This rationale fully motivates the meta‑cognitive loop and aligns with decades of research on chunking in cognitive architectures (e.g., SOAR, ACT‑R) and on options in hierarchical reinforcement learning.

